Wisdom is what counts for monks, not intelligence.

> The point
> At 23000 turns, I had him reread the sleep spellbook to avoid any bad 
> results with casting a poorly remembered spell. Around 28000, I noticed 
> that his spell difficulty had increased to 58% from 0%.

Increased from 58 to 0? I guess that you mean that the failure chance 
had increased from 58% to 100%. Or did the success chance decrease from 
58% to 0%?

> I moved him to a safe level, stripped him down and attempted to cast
> sleep and saw that it was still 58%!

You stripped him down. Did that include removing a robe? Monks have at 
least double the spell success improvement effect for wearing a robe 
compared to any other class.

> What causes this? Heck, he was even maxed out in the enchantment spell 
> skill!

That means he was at basic level.

Here I give some heavily simplified formulas derived from Kevin and 
Dylan's spoiler at http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/spl1-343.txt - 
I'm rather sure there are errors in my simplification, but the general 
idea is to show 1) how much you benefit from robe, and 2) how much 
wisdom matters:

Success chance in percent of level 1 spell at basic skill level for a 
stripped monk:
~ 4.4*wisdom - 8  (clipped to 0-100)

Success chance in percent of level 1 spell at basic skill level for a 
stripped-but-robed monk:
~ 11,73*wisdom + 54,67  (clipped to 0-100)

To get an exact formula we would need to know the experience level and 
wisdom.

Hmm, I see that these formulas doesn't match up well with your reported 
figures. I blame that on my miscalculations, and advice you to check the 
spoiler yourself. :)

